To place an SVG icon (like a search/reading glass icon) on the right side of an input and make it clickable, you can use HTML and CSS. Here's how you can achieve this:

### HTML:
```html
<div class="input-container">
  <input type="text" id="searchInput" placeholder="Search..." />
  <button class="search-button" onclick="handleSearch()">
    <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
      <circle cx="11" cy="11" r="8"></circle>
      <line x1="21" y1="21" x2="16.65" y2="16.65"></line>
    </svg>
  </button>
</div>
```

### CSS:
```css
.input-container {
  position: relative;
  display: inline-block;
  width: 300px; /* Adjust as needed */
}

.input-container input {
  width: 100%;
  padding-right: 30px; /* Make space for the icon */
  box-sizing: border-box;
}

.search-button {
  position: absolute;
  right: 0;
  top: 50%;
  transform: translateY(-50%);
  background: none;
  border: none;
  cursor: pointer;
  padding: 5px;
}

.search-button svg {
  display: block;
}
```

### JavaScript:
```javascript
function handleSearch() {
  const inputValue = document.getElementById('searchInput').value;
  console.log('Searching for:', inputValue);
  // Your search logic here
}
```

### Explanation:
1. **HTML Structure**:
   - The input and button are wrapped in a `div` for positioning.
   - The button contains the SVG icon (a simple magnifying glass).

2. **CSS**:
   - The container is `relative` so the button can be positioned absolutely inside it.
   - The input has `padding-right` to prevent text from being hidden under the icon.
   - The button is absolutely positioned to the right and vertically centered.

3. **JavaScript**:
   - The `handleSearch` function is called when the button is clicked. It gets the input's value and can perform any search logic you need.

### Alternative Approach (Pure SVG Icon):
If you want to style the SVG directly without a button, you can make the SVG clickable:
```html
<div class="input-container">
  <input type="text" id="searchInput" placeholder="Search..." />
  <svg class="search-icon" onclick="handleSearch()" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
    <circle cx="11" cy="11" r="8"></circle>
    <line x1="21" y1="21" x2="16.65" y2="16.65"></line>
  </svg>
</div>
```

CSS for this approach:
```css
.search-icon {
  position: absolute;
  right: 10px;
  top: 50%;
  transform: translateY(-50%);
  cursor: pointer;
}
```

This will give you a clean, functional search input with a clickable icon.

